# Lesson 3
## Key features of X-linked inheritance
Genes on X and Y chromosomes are distributed unequally

There are few Y-linked genes, but >300 genes associated with X-linked disease phenotypes

These phenotypes have a characteristic sex
distribution. We expect to see:
  ▪ A preponderance of disease in AMAB
  ▪ Affected AMAB with no affected offspring, but all their
daughters are carriers
  ▪ No male-to-male transmission
  ▪ A significant proportion of isolated cases due to new
mutation

## Recessive or Dominant?
* Typically distinguished based on the phenotype in
heterozygous AFAB
  - Consistently apparent clinically = dominant
  - Phenotype not typically observed = recessive
▪ Random X inactivation makes these rules hard to follow

▪ There is mosaic expression of X-linked genes

## Hemophilia A - X-linked recessive
