## DMD - X-link recessive (mild symptom on carrier tho)
▪ DMD encodes dystrophin expressed in smooth, skeletal,
and cardiac muscles
  - Typically see large deletions in one or two hot spots
  - 2/3 inherited, 1/3 de novo if isolated case
▪ Normal muscle tone at birth, but boys develop
weakness in the proximal muscles over time
  - See gait abnormalities between 3 and 5 years of age

▪ Gower maneuver used to rise from a sitting or prone
position by 5 years of age

▪ Confined to wheelchair by 12 years of age

▪ Many reach adulthood (20s), but it is life-threatening

▪ 95% have some cardiac compromise

## Rett Syndrome
Rett syndrome is a rare, severe genetic neurodevelopmental disorder
that primarily affects girls, causing a progressive loss of motor skills,
purposeful hand use, and spoken language after a period of typical early 
development.

## X-inactivation
▪ Used to be called Lyon hypothesis: “lyonization” based on
geneticist Mary Lyon who proposed 
  - Proposed that during early embryonic non germ cells, one of two
X chromosomes is randomly inactivated
  - That specific X chromosome is permanently inactivated in that
cell and all cell progeny

▪ Under control of XIST gene- expressed from otherwise inactive
X chromosome and shuts off rest of X chromosome

▪ AFAB is a mosaic: X is inactivated randomly: same X chr will be
inactivated in some cells and active in others

▪ In AFAB who have deletion on one of their X chr, the abnormal
X chr may be inactivated, or where it is inactivated in the body
helps to determine severity - however, this can change when
thinking about translocations (will discuss in cyto units)

